<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  launchd agent for the weekly damage-panel publish (macOS).

  Install:
    cp bin/com.mspinola.crowdmon-publish.plist.example \
       ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mspinola.crowdmon-publish.plist
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mspinola.crowdmon-publish.plist

  Uninstall:
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mspinola.crowdmon-publish.plist

  Run it once by hand first and read the log. It should report 47 markets and a report
  week matching the latest CFTC release:
    bin/publish_damage.sh --dry-run

  Schedule: 09:30 local, daily, fifteen minutes after com.mspinola.crowdmon-live-tests.

  ORDER IS THE POINT, not the hour. The live-test run at 09:15 is the thing that would
  already have alarmed if the store were mid-write or this package broken, and it takes
  around 70 seconds. Publishing after it means a panel is never written from a tree whose
  own suite is failing. Both are clear of the cotdata overnight sync (observed landing
  ~06:25) and of com.mspinola.cotdata-verify at 08:45, so a failure here is about this
  package rather than about a store that had not arrived yet. Neither collides with
  npf-weekly-setups (Fri 17:00), npf-orchestrate (Fri 18:00) or livebook-daily
  (weekdays 07:30).

  DAILY, THOUGH THE DATA IS WEEKLY, and that is deliberate. The panel is anchored on the
  report date: `add_trigger_distance` takes `as_of` from the frame's latest report_date,
  not from today, so a re-run between releases reproduces the same numbers and rewrites the
  same dated directory. It is idempotent, and the repetition buys the thing that matters:
  **a failed publish repairs itself the next morning instead of leaving a stale panel up
  for a week.** On a weekly schedule one bad Friday is seven days of a dashboard showing
  the wrong week, which the consuming page can only report, not fix.

  UNLIKE the live-test agent, this one WRITES. Two consequences follow.

  First, the failure that matters is not an exception. A run that reads the store while it
  is being written produces a SHORT panel, which is a perfectly well-formed panel that
  nothing downstream would question; `bin/live-tests.sh` documents the observed incident
  (2026-08-03, a run reporting 480 passed / 12 skipped against the usual 487 / 5). The
  guard is `publish._refuse_a_short_panel`, which refuses a market count below 80% of the
  previous publish and exits non-zero, so that case arrives as this notification rather
  than as a quietly wrong dashboard.

  Second, a failure here is louder than it looks in the consumer. cot-analyzer's /damage
  page renders a "not available" card and a staleness banner rather than an error, which is
  the right behaviour and also means **nobody finds out from the dashboard**. This
  notification is the only channel that says the publisher stopped.

  The log is append-only beside the live-test log, same convention.

  FIRST RUN FAILED, AND THE REASON IS WORTH KEEPING. The agent points at an absolute path
  into the MAIN checkout, which had not been pulled after the publisher merged, so it died
  on "No such file or directory" while the branch that added it sat green in a worktree.
  That is `CLAUDE.md`'s editable-install hazard 5 arriving as a scheduled-job failure rather
  than a test failure: the worktree and the checkout are different trees, and only one of
  them is what launchd runs. Anything scheduled here runs the MAIN checkout at whatever
  commit it happens to be on, so merging a change is not the same as deploying it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.mspinola.crowdmon-publish</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>{ echo "===== $(date) ====="; /Users/matts/code/trading_workspace/crowdmon/bin/publish_damage.sh; rc=$?; echo; } &gt;&gt; /Users/matts/code/trading_workspace/crowdmon/publish.log 2&gt;&amp;1; [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || osascript -e 'display notification "damage panel publish FAILED — see crowdmon/publish.log" with title "crowdmon" sound name "Basso"'</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>
            <key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
